m4_changequote()
kathara-lclean(1) -- Stop a Kathara network scenario
=============================================

## SYNOPSIS

`kathara lclean` [`-h`] [`-d` <DIRECTORY> \| `--lab-name` <LAB_NAME>] [`--exclude` <DEVICE_NAME> [<DEVICE_NAME> ...]] [<DEVICE_NAME> [<DEVICE_NAME> ...]]  

## DESCRIPTION

Gracefully shut down a running network scenario.
By default, stops all the devices of the network scenario. If a list of <DEVICE_NAME>s is provided, then only devices with a matching name and that are part of the network scenario will be halted. 

## OPTIONS

* `-h`, `--help`:
    Show a help message and exit.

* `-d` <DIRECTORY>, `--directory` <DIRECTORY>:
	Specify the folder containing the network scenario.

	Cleans the Kathara network scenario that is located inside <DIRECTORY>.  
	If no `-d` option is provided, assume the network scenario is located in the current directory.

* `--lab-name` <LAB_NAME>:
    Specify the name of the network scenario containing the specified device.

    Only works for a network scenario started with a name, i.e., one that declares `LAB_NAME` in `kathara-lab.conf`(5) or that was started through the Kathara Python APIs.
    Otherwise, the network scenario is identified by its path, and must be selected with `-d` or `--directory`.

    Settings in the `kathara.conf`(5) file of the network scenario directory are not loaded. If it changes `manager_type` or `remote_url`, the network scenario cannot be found by name.

    This option cannot be used in conjunction with `-d` or `--directory`.

* `--exclude` [<DEVICE_NAME> [<DEVICE_NAME> ...]]
    A list of device names. You can exclude certain devices of the network scenario from being shut down.

* `DEVICE_NAME`:
    A list of device names. Instead of shutting down the whole network scenario, only specified devices are stopped.

